comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T14:40:25.095Z","harness:codex","Scope selected after all-status and GitHub intake: co-deliver pm-xm0id4 and pm-2ew0w3 because both expose CLI-private lifecycle policy that must become SDK-owned. No other item is in progress."
  "2026-07-31T15:21:34.990Z","harness:codex","Delivery evidence: close transaction ownership moved under src/sdk/lifecycle; lifecycle policy is public, individually testable, selectable by SDK consumers, and shared by CLI/MCP. The CLI close module is now a compatibility shim; public surface snapshot records 24 classified additive exports. Fresh npm and Bun consumers installed the packed 2026.7.31 tarball, imported closeItem and policy primitives from @unbrained/pm-cli/sdk, and ran contained npx/bunx binaries. Full coverage passed 6,610 tests at 100/100/100/100. Remaining create/update/delete/restore/context SDK-to-CLI inversions mean the broad item is intentionally not complete in this PR."
  "2026-07-31T15:49:45.896Z","harness:codex",The generated error-code catalog was refreshed after moving close implementation ownership; close-family error source provenance now names sdk/lifecycle/close.ts rather than the CLI shim.
  "2026-07-31T16:10:53.522Z","harness:codex","Review loop evidence: regenerated the full runtime contract snapshot; canonicalized close target ids; shared one tracker scan across active-child and auto-unblock analysis; and verified 6,613 tests at 100/100/100/100 plus static quality. CI root cause was only a stale generated contract fixture."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T14:28:52.007Z","harness:claude-code","Counting import directions between two layers gives a precise answer to an architectural question that usually gets a qualitative one. Here 86 CLI-to-SDK imports against 36 SDK-to-CLI imports across 3 files reads as nearly-done until the 36 are enumerated and turn out to be the entire item lifecycle - so the useful metric is which capabilities flow the wrong way, not how many files do."
